```c
guess = htmlFindEncoding(ctxt);   // line 443
if (guess == NULL) {
    xmlSwitchEncoding(ctxt, XML_CHAR_ENCODING_8859_1);
} else {
    handler = xmlFindCharEncodingHandler((const char *) guess);
    xmlFree(guess);   // line 448 - frees guess
    if (handler != NULL) {
        /*
         * Don't use UTF-8 encoder which isn't required and
         * can produce invalid UTF-8.
         */
        if (!xmlStrEqual(BAD_CAST handler->name, BAD_CAST "UTF-8"))
            xmlSwitchToEncoding(ctxt, handler);
    } else {
        htmlParseErr(ctxt, XML_ERR_INVALID_ENCODING,
                     "Unsupported encoding %s", guess, NULL);   // line 457-458 - uses freed guess
    }
}
```

So the bug is:
1. `htmlFindEncoding()` returns a heap-allocated string (the encoding name from the meta tag)
2. `xmlFindCharEncodingHandler((const char *) guess)` is called - if it returns NULL (encoding not found/unsupported), then
3. `xmlFree(guess)` frees the buffer
4. `htmlParseErr(ctxt, XML_ERR_INVALID_ENCODING, "Unsupported encoding %s", guess, NULL)` passes the freed `guess` as str1
5. `htmlParseErr` → `__xmlRaiseError` → `xmlStrdup(str1)` reads freed memory

Wait, let me re-read. The order is:
```c
handler = xmlFindCharEncodingHandler((const char *) guess);
xmlFree(guess);   // freed here
if (handler != NULL) {
    ...
} else {
    htmlParseErr(ctxt, XML_ERR_INVALID_ENCODING,
                 "Unsupported encoding %s", guess, NULL);   // use-after-free here
